NAME
       vim, vi, view - the standard screen text editor

SYNOPSIS
       vim [+N] [FILE]...
       vi [FILE]...
       view FILE...

DESCRIPTION
       vim (Vi IMproved) edits text files in the terminal. It is on every
       RHEL system, which makes it the editor to know: on a minimal install
       vi may be the only one. On RHEL 9, vi is the small vim from
       vim-minimal; the vim command comes from vim-enhanced. view opens a
       file read-only.

       vim has modes, which is what surprises newcomers. It starts in normal
       mode, where keys are commands, not text. Press i to enter insert mode
       and type; press Esc to go back to normal mode. Type : in normal mode
       for a command line at the bottom (to save, quit, search and replace).
       When lost, press Esc (twice does no harm).

   Leaving
       :w        save                 :wq or :x or ZZ   save and quit
       :q        quit (refused when there are unsaved changes)
       :q!       quit and throw the changes away
       :w FILE   save under another name

   Entering insert mode
       i, a      insert before, or after, the cursor
       I, A      insert at the start, or end, of the line
       o, O      open a new line below, or above, and insert
       R         overwrite characters until Esc

   Moving (normal mode)
       h j k l   left, down, up, right (the arrow keys work too)
       w, b      next word, previous word
       0, ^, $   start of line, first non-blank, end of line
       gg, G     first line, last line; 15G goes to line 15
       Ctrl+F, Ctrl+B
                 a screen forward, back (Page Down, Page Up)

   Changing (normal mode)
       x         delete the character under the cursor
       dd        delete (cut) the line; 3dd deletes three lines
       dw, D     delete to the end of the word, of the line
       cw, C     change to the end of the word, of the line
       yy        copy (yank) the line; 5yy copies five lines
       p, P      paste after, or before, the cursor
       r C       replace one character with C
       J         join the next line to this one
       u         undo; Ctrl+R redoes
       .         repeat the last change
       V         select whole lines; then d, y or > act on them

   Searching and replacing
       /TEXT     search forward; ?TEXT searches back
       n, N      next, previous match
       :s/OLD/NEW/         replace the first OLD on this line
       :%s/OLD/NEW/g       replace every OLD in the file
       :noh                remove the search highlight

   Settings
       :set number (:set nu)   show line numbers; :set nonu hides them
       :syntax on              colour the text by file type

OPTIONS
       +N     Start at line N (+ alone: the last line).

       -R     Read-only, like view.

EXAMPLES
       Edit the SSH server settings:

           vim /etc/ssh/sshd_config

       A typical change: find a line, change it, save:

           /PermitRootLogin      (Enter: jump to it)
           cw ... Esc             (change the word)
           :wq                    (save and quit)

       Look at a log without risking a change:

           view /var/log/messages

NOTES
       vimtutor is a 30-minute interactive lesson that teaches the basics.
       For crontab -e, visudo, vipw and systemctl edit, the editor is chosen
       by $EDITOR or $VISUAL (vi by default).

FILES
       ~/.vimrc
              Your own settings, read at start.

       /etc/vimrc
              Settings for everyone.

SEE ALSO
       vimtutor(1), nano(1), sed(1)
